# ---- repo files that are not part of the site ----
# Netlify publishes the repo root, so build inputs shipped with it. Two problems:
# the 16 fragments under scripts/category-bodies and scripts/tool-seo are headless
# copies of real page bodies (duplicate content Google can index against the
# originals), and the .ps1 files disclose local filesystem paths and the name of
# the Icecat credentials file. No secrets are in them -- .gitignore keeps the
# .env files out and they 404 -- but none of this belongs on the public site.
#
# Forced (!) because these files EXIST: an unforced rule is shadowed by a real
# static file, which is exactly the case here.
#
# /netlify/* is the SOURCE path only. The live function endpoint is
# /.netlify/functions/* (dot-prefixed) and is unaffected -- verified before
# adding this.
# 301 rather than 404 on purpose. Netlify's force flag does NOT let a 404 rule
# shadow a file that actually exists -- tested against the live site: the same
# rules written as `404!` left every one of these paths returning 200, while a
# 404! on a path with no file behind it worked (which proves the file was being
# read, not the rule ignored). A forced 301 does take precedence, so these paths
# now bounce to the homepage: the fragment content stops being served, which is
# what both the duplicate-content and the disclosure problem needed.
/scripts/*         /   301!
/netlify/*         /   301!
/app-wrapper/*     /   301!
/README.md         /   301!
/APP-STORES.md     /   301!
/package.json      /   301!
/package-lock.json /   301!

